A||||4||Cheever3000||Clutch line fix question||||5||
Z||000000||Cheever3000||11-26-2001||08:57 AM||||The hydraulic line under the driver's side of my coupe was broken on a trailer ramp.  I am hoping to use a small length of hose for replacing the broken part of the tube.  Will it handle the pressure?  <B>Must</B> I replace the entire solid tube (gosh I hope not!)<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||MrPBody||11-26-2001||09:08 AM||||Although clutch line pressure probably is not as great as brake line pressure, I don't think a clamped hose will work long, if it works at all.  However, you could splice-in a piece of steel tube with flare or compression fittings.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||11-26-2001||09:13 AM||||I wouldn't recommend using a rubber line for a splice. Clean up the ends that broke so that they are square. You now have an option, using a double flaring tool to flare both ends and inserting a splice from after market brake line using flare fittings OR using compression fittings and inserting a piece of brake line to complete the run.<BR>I recommend the first suggestion, more dependable.<P>MRPBODY IS quick this morning!<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 11-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||11-26-2001||01:38 PM||||clean the line and compression fittings work nice.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wade87GT||11-26-2001||02:20 PM||||I replaced my entire line which wasn't too difficult really. Especially after I removed the spare tire well. I bought the new line from <A HREF="http://www.gmpartsdirect.com" TARGET=_blank>www.gmpartsdirect.com</A>  at a good price. I'm not at home right now so I don't have the part number or price available.<P>Wade||||reg||1
